#Viewing Files

Command: cat file.txt
Function: To read text in files
Example:
root@ubuntu:/home$ echo "hello world" > file.txt
root@ubuntu:/home$ cat file.txt
hello world

Command: cat /etc/shells
Function: To know what SHELLS you have available
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ cat /etc/shells
# /etc/shells: valid login shells
/bin/sh
/usr/bin/sh
/bin/bash
/usr/bin/bash
/bin/rbash
/usr/bin/rbash
/usr/bin/dash
/usr/bin/screen
/usr/bin/tmux

Command: grep "TEXT" > file.txt
Function: To find specific word in files
Example:
root@ubuntu:/home$ grep "hello" file.txt
hello world

Command: echo "TEXT" > file.txt
Function: To add text to files (>> to add additional text to files)
Example:
root@ubuntu:/home$ echo "hello world" > file.txt
root@ubuntu:/home$ cat file.txt
hello world
root@ubuntu:/home$ grep "hello" file.txt
hello world
root@ubuntu:/home$ echo "test" >> file.txt
root@ubuntu:/home$ cat file.txt
hello world
test

Command: echo $
Function: Used to show SHELL or PATH
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ echo $SHELL
/bin/bash
root@ubuntu:~$ echo $PATH
/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/local/go/bin:/
snap/bin
root@ubuntu:~$ PATH tells the SHELL where it needs to look in the different bina
ries

Command: vim file.txt
Function: Text editor (if you have permission)
Example with navigation keys:
root@ubuntu:~$ vim example.txt
root@ubuntu:~$ cat example.txt
This is an example text file.
You can write anything here.
press 'i' to insert text.
press 'esc' to enter command mode.
In command mode, use 'h' to move right.
'l' to move left
'k' to move up.
'j' to move down.
To save and exit enter command mode then type ':wq!'.
If you make change and don't want to save then you enter ':q!'.

Navigation keys continued:
'0' to move to the beginning of the line.
'*' to move to the end of the line.
'w' to move across word by word.
'b' to move backwards word by word.
':' followed by line number e.g. '4' to move to a specific line.
'/' followed by a specific word to find it.
'n' to find the next occurrence on the previous step.
'N' to go back to previous occurrence.
'dd' to delete a whole line must be next to the line.
'D' to delete a section from the line.
'y' to copy (yank) a line.
'p' to paste a line.
'u' to undo previous action.
'ctrl r' to redo the undone change
':syntax' to highlight commands
':set number' to add numbers to each line
':set nonumber' to remove numbers for each line
':w' to save without exiting.

Command: head (-n NUMBER)
Function: To view the first ten lines of a file, use -n NUMBER to specify lines.
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ touch multiline.txt
root@ubuntu:~$ vim multiline.txt
root@ubuntu:~$ head multiline.txt
Line 1: This is the first line.
Line 2: This is the second line.
Line 3: This is the third line.
Line 4: This is the fourth line.
Line 5: This is the fifth line.
Line 6: This is the sixth line.
Line 7: This is the seventh line.
Line 8: This is the eighth line.
Line 9: This is the ninth line.
Line 10: This is the tenth line.
root@ubuntu:~$ head -n 5 multiline.txt
Line 1: This is the first line.
Line 2: This is the second line.
Line 3: This is the third line.
Line 4: This is the fourth line.
Line 5: This is the fifth line.

Command: tail (-n NUMBER)
Function: To view the last ten lines of a file, use -n NUMBER to specify lines.
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ tail multiline.txt
Line 6: This is the sixth line.
Line 7: This is the seventh line.
Line 8: This is the eighth line.
Line 9: This is the ninth line.
Line 10: This is the tenth line.
Line 11: This is the eleventh line.
Line 12: This is the twelfth line.
Line 13: This is the thirteenth line.
Line 14: This is the fourteenth line.
Line 15: This is the fifteenth line.
root@ubuntu:~$ tail -n 5 multiline.txt
Line 11: This is the eleventh line.
Line 12: This is the twelfth line.
Line 13: This is the thirteenth line.
Line 14: This is the fourteenth line.
Line 15: This is the fifteenth line.

Command: head -n 10 file.txt | tail -n 5
Function: To view the a specific portion of lines in a file.
Example:
root@ubuntu:~$ head -n 10 multiline.txt | tail -n 5
Line 6: This is the sixth line.
Line 7: This is the seventh line.
Line 8: This is the eighth line.
Line 9: This is the ninth line.
Line 10: This is the tenth line.

